RISC-V: a call that never returns, ending a throw block, becomes a fast tail call. Applied after perf-48; JIT only.
- A method whose only calls are fast tail calls already drops its RA save, and with it often its whole frame
  (perf-31/42). A method that only calls out to throw - `ThrowHelper.ThrowX()` behind a guard - keeps both,
  for a call that never comes back.
- Lowering::TryTailCallNoReturn turns such a call into a fast tail call: the block becomes BBJ_RETURN with
  BBF_HAS_JMP, so the epilog restores the callee-saved registers and pops the frame before the jump. An
  exception the callee throws then unwinds straight into our caller, where it would go anyway from a method
  without EH clauses. The frame is gone while the callee runs, so the method may have no address-exposed or
  pinned local and every argument must travel in a register; synchronized, P/Invoke-frame, GS-cookie,
  localloc, varargs, async, profiled and unoptimized methods are left alone, as are virtual, unmanaged,
  delegate, R2R and non-standard-argument calls.
- Only a stack trace would miss the frame, so it follows RiscV64ElideLeafRaSave, the zkVM-guest knob whose
  RA elision it feeds.

